It's good and bad. It's more good than bad, especially in comparison to other decks.
The whole "nasal g" thing seems to be made almost on purpose, to the point where ti sounds ridiculous. During immersion it's not this overtly abused, but maybe it's my impression, you get used to it anyway and you need to get used to it anyway.

The images make no sense. The image has nothing to do with the sample sentence. Yeah sure it expresses the same concept, but why do I have a sample sentence about somebody touching your shoulder suddenly at night, to then see the picture of a car supposedly crashing?

They are correlated to the word itself, but often completely detatched from the sample sentence itself. And since words are kanji heavy (which is a good thing) you cannot rely on your understanding of the context of the sentence, unless you already know how to read it. And the image is, you know, useless, if not confusing.

The good thing is that the sorting is exceptionally well done. Words here are commonly heard and are ordered in a way that does make sense. Furthermore, it seems like some type of effort to replicate an artificial i+1 on the sample sentences was done.

Better than many other when it comes to the learning itself, but you will have some very painful and exhausting Anki sessions.
