¯¢º¡Ó"¤Sadly, this is the best deck you can find. I have a few things to say about it:

1) Word order is generally not bad, but there are unnecessarily many introductory words.
2) Kanji knowledge is not built up sequentially, but rather randomly. A good example from this deck would be the order: ä»Š â†’ ä»Šæ—¥ â†’ ä»Šå› â†’ ä»Šæœ â†’ ä»Šå¾Œ. It starts with "now" and then extends this concept to days and year, and finally to the future and something more abstract. Bad examples: å…ƒæ°— â†’ æ°—æŒã¡ â†’ æ°— and ä¸Šã’ã‚‹ â†’ ä¸Š. Such patterns help with memorizing words, as they are not just random concepts floating in empty space.
3) Sentences start as N+1, but then stop for some reason. Thus, they are kind of useless. Sentences tend to show less obvious use cases, but not knowing a word or two makes them incomprehensible. I decided to turn sentences on the front into hints and never used them again.
4) There are duplicates. If I forget that there are two meanings and only recall one of them, does this count as a correct answer? With sentences, you would, of course, be able to tell the difference, but then would you be memorizing a word based on its writing or the sentence below? Maybe combining such cards into one would help, but I have no idea how to combine different pronunciations.

As a community deck, Kaishi has a lot of potential. I give this review as my small contribution to the project and hope it will become better.
