I've used a lot of different Japanese vocab decks before. Some of them are really popular, others not so much. But out of everything Iâ€™ve tried so far, the KAISHI 1.5k deck is honestly the one I like the most.

The reason is simple: this deck forces you to actually learn the words properly if you want to make progress. The cards are kept super clean and minimal, which helps a lot because thereâ€™s no distracting extra info all over the place. You just focus on the vocab and the example sentences.

What I highly recommend is reading the example sentences out loud, trying to translate them yourself first, and only then flipping the card. If you do that, youâ€™ll not only remember the words faster, but youâ€™ll also get a solid feeling for how theyâ€™re used naturally in real sentences.

In my opinion, this is one of the best decks out there for building real, usable vocab. Sure, sometimes there are words that you probably wonâ€™t hear often or at all - but that shouldnâ€™t discourage anyone. The deck still contains a ton of super common, everyday words.

Honestly, even after the first 10 to 200 words, youâ€™ll already start noticing them constantly when watching japanese stuff. Sometimes every two to three minutes youâ€™ll hear something you already know, and thatâ€™s super motivating.
