With a few addons, it becomes the ultimate starting deck. I use Review Heatmap, Japanese Pitch Accent (is more visual for me, I put it on the same list to get used to this deck pitch accent writing because it's the one used in most books), and Kanji Grid Kuuube.

To any hesitant person, you WILL progress.
I had to reset my laptop, Anki destroyed my backup (no sound, no image) so I had to restart the deck today (deleted my deck and re downloaded this one).
I was at 250 cards, since November (wasn't regular every day, became serious in January). Went for 5-10 cards/day, and idk how but re adding the deck put the cards by order of difficulty. I've put 150 cards/day and finished them in 5-10 minutes.
Was happily surprised by it. Especially since I wasn't studying Japanese on the side, due to working a 9-5 job and playing video games in my free time.

My tip would be to challenge yourself but to not ignore your mental capacity (happened to me to avoid studying the deck for like two weeks, sometime a good mental rest is needed). Don't hesitate to set your cards/day high at first, then to reduce it if not stop adding new cards until you're comfortable or have huge gaps between your repetitions.
