Ç–¿ó»"¼Highly recommended! This might be the best pre-made starter deck for Japanese learners

Compared to 4 other recommended starter decks, it was easily the best to me because of:
â€¢ Word Selection and Progression: The order of cards follows a logical progression suited to any beginner. Words are selected based on frequency, so you're guaranteed to encounter them often in your Japanese immersion. Overall, the deck is good preparation for a variety of Japanese content
    â€¢ Kaishi's early progression was much better than the other deck I primarily used, as that deck didn't group related words and also introduced many hard-to-learn abstract words early on. Kaishi focuses more on simpler words at the start and very importantly - groups related words together, making them easier to learn and create associations
â€¢ Pictures: every card has a picture on the back which makes the deck more visually engaging. Plus all the images have a consistent visual style (as they're sourced from a single site)
â€¢ Audio: spoken audio for Words AND Example sentences. A variety of voices are used, and 99% of them have the same high level of quality/clarity - so you won't get tired of hearing the same voice like in other decks 
	- note: Be aware of the rare card in which 'g' is pronounced as 'ng' (e.g. cards for ã‚ã’ã‚‹, å‹•ã, ã™ã”ã„). This is called a "nasal" g sound, which is a less common pronuncation but technically still correct as natives do use them
â€¢ Template: great template out-of-the-box. Plus, the simple template and modular fields make it easy to modify to your preferences
